

In Spring, scopes define the lifecycle and visibility of beans within the Spring container.
By default, Spring beans are singleton, meaning there's only one instance of the bean per Spring IoC container.


Singleton: One instance per Spring IoC container (default).
Prototype: A new instance for every request.
Request: One instance per HTTP request (web application).
Session: One instance per HTTP session (web application).
Application: One instance per ServletContext (web application).
WebSocket: One instance per WebSocket session (web application).

Singleton (@Scope("singleton")): Only one instance of the bean is created for the entire application context. This is the default scope.
Prototype (@Scope("prototype")): A new instance of the bean is created each time it is requested.
Request (@Scope("request")): A new instance is created for each HTTP request. Typically used in web applications.
Session (@Scope("session")): A new instance is created for each HTTP session. Used in web applications.
Application (@Scope("application")): A single instance is created for the entire lifecycle of a ServletContext.
WebSocket (@Scope("websocket")): A single instance is created for the lifecycle of a WebSocket.


👉 @Scope is used at the class level, not method level.but on @Been

🔍 What does @Scope do?
The @Scope annotation in Spring tells the container how long the bean should live, and how it should be created or reused.

✅ Correct usage (on a class):
java
Copy
Edit
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class MyBean {
    public MyBean() {
        System.out.println("New instance created!");
    }
}
Every time this bean is injected or requested — a new instance will be created because it's prototype.

❌ Invalid usage (on a method):
java
Copy
Edit
public class MyConfig {
    @Scope("prototype") // ❌ This has no effect here
    public MyBean myBean() {
        return new MyBean();
    }
}
The above won’t work unless you use @Bean along with @Scope in a configuration class:

✅ Valid usage with @Bean (in config class):
java
Copy
Edit
@Configuration
public class MyConfig {

    @Bean
    @Scope("prototype") // ✅ Valid on @Bean methods
    public MyBean myBean() {
        return new MyBean();
    }
}
✅ Summary
| Where to Use `@Scope`    | Valid? | Notes                                     |
| ------------------------ | ------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| On a `@Component` class  | ✅      | Common usage                              |
| On a method with `@Bean` | ✅      | Valid when used in `@Configuration` class |
| On a regular method      | ❌      | Has no effect                             |





🔍 Example: Singleton vs Prototype

@Component
@Scope("singleton") // default
public class MyService {
    // lives as long as the app context lives
}

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class MyService {
    // a new instance created every time it’s injected
}


🔁 Lifespan Summary:
| Scope         | Created When?           | Lives Until?                |
| ------------- | ----------------------- | --------------------------- |
| `singleton`   | App context starts      | App context ends            |
| `prototype`   | Every injection/request | Garbage collected after use |
| `request`     | On each HTTP request    | When request ends           |
| `session`     | When session starts     | When session ends           |
| `application` | App context starts      | App context ends            |
| `websocket`   | WebSocket session opens | WebSocket session closes    |


💡 Best Practices:
Use singleton for stateless services (most common).

Use prototype for stateful or expensive objects.

Use request/session only in web layers, and avoid them in service/repo layers.

Don’t mix scopes without caution—injecting a prototype into a singleton may not work as expected unless you handle it properly (e.g., with ObjectFactory or Provider).